text:
The model was built in five steps, and each step is based on certain assumptions: 1-Set requirements and scenarios: a static, macroscopic, multimodal model on four-stage logic, covering the region and the Strait, able to zoom into main urban areas, for strategic planning. Six scenarios were defined by period, day type and time band;
2- Build zones and supply: municipalities were zoned by size and demographic informations, giving 997 internal zones, 46 external zones and 122 Park & Ride zones. The road graph and rail graph were completed with GTFS timetables for bus, rail and ferry and with fare systems;
3-Reconstruct the demand: demand is reconstructed starting from the mobile-phone O-D matrix and segmented into students, long-distance trips (over 30 km) and short-distance trips (under 30 km). Student trips are estimated separately, based on school locations and enrolment and on the school-age population. Matrices for each time band are derived from the peak matrix using the daily profile from phone data, and heavy-goods vehicle trips are added using floating-car data; 
4-Split by mode and assign: a logit model shares demand between car, public transport and Park & Ride using time, cost, access, waiting, walking, transfers and fares, with different coefficients depending on demand segment (students, trips over 30 km, trips under 30 km) and period (winter, summer). The model is run separately for each time band. Cars are assigned by equilibrium, so no user gains by changing route. Public transport uses the timetable-based procedure, which captures waiting times and transfers between services;
5-Calibrate and validate: mode-choice parameters start from the literature and good practice and were tuned against expected shares, line boardings and road counts. Model fit was assessed using R² and GEH against observed road traffic and boarding data.
